%[text] # Arrays and Plots: Represent a physical model and compare prediction with evidence
%[text] **LECTURE DEMONSTRATION**
%[text] PHY4605 Physics-First Reproducible MATLAB Notebook
%%
%[text] ## Physical Question and Prediction
%[text] A ball is launched straight upward. Before computing, predict the shape of its vertical-position graph during the first four seconds: it should rise, slow to a highest point, then fall.
%[text] **Learning outcomes.** By the end of this demonstration, you can identify variables and units in a familiar model, evaluate that model over an array, and read a labelled plot with one validation check.
%%
%[text] ## Model, Assumptions, and Units
%[text] We choose upward as positive. Air resistance is neglected and gravity is constant. In words: position equals the starting position plus the upward launch contribution minus the downward gravity contribution.
%[text] The analytical model is $y(t)=y\_0+v\_0t-\\frac{1}{2}gt^2$. Here `y_m` is position in m, `t_s` is time in s, `y0_m` is the starting position in m, `v0_mps` is launch velocity in m s^-1, and `g_mps2` is gravitational acceleration in m s^-2.
%[text] This code assigns meaningful scalar parameters. Read the units in the variable names before running the section.
y0_m = 0;          % starting vertical position (m)
v0_mps = 20;       % upward launch velocity (m s^-1)
g_mps2 = 9.81;     % gravitational acceleration magnitude (m s^-2)
%%
%[text] ## Time as an Array
%[text] A scalar holds one value; an array holds many values. The colon expression below starts at 0 s, advances by 0.1 s, and ends at 4 s. MATLAB indexing starts at 1, so `t_s(1)` is the first stored time.
%[text] Run this section and inspect the three displayed values: the array length, its first value, and its eleventh value.
t_s = 0:0.1:4;     % time samples from 0 s to 4 s
n_samples = length(t_s) %[output:3c022678]
first_time_s = t_s(1) %[output:5d1ca851]
eleventh_time_s = t_s(11) %[output:824e8f1e]
%%
%[text] ## Algorithm Before Code
%[text] **Pseudocode:** (1) choose the physical parameters; (2) make an array of times; (3) calculate position at every time; (4) plot position against time; (5) check the known starting value; (6) explain the graph.
%[text] In the equation, `*`, `/`, and `^` are for scalar operations. Because `t_s` is an array, MATLAB needs `.*`, `./`, and `.^` when an operation is applied element by element. The dot means: do this to every time sample.
%%
%[text] ## Evaluate the Model at Every Time Sample
%[text] This short calculation applies the familiar equation to all 41 entries of `t_s`. The two dots are essential: `.*` multiplies each time sample by `v0_mps`, and `.^2` squares each time sample.
y_m = y0_m + v0_mps.*t_s - 0.5*g_mps2.*t_s.^2;
position_at_start_m = y_m(1) %[output:9b5b454a]
position_at_1s_m = y_m(11) %[output:25056a70]
%%
%[text] ## Plot the Physical Output
%[text] The horizontal axis is time in s; the vertical axis is vertical position in m. Run the section, then compare the plotted curve with your prediction before computing.
plot_handle = plot(nan,nan,'LineWidth',2.5,'Color','red','Marker','^');
grid on
xlabel('Time, t (s)')
ylabel('Vertical position, y (m)')
title('Ball Launched Upward: Position Against Time')
xlim([min(t_s) max(t_s)])
ylim([min(y_m) max(y_m)])

for sample_index = 1:numel(t_s)
    set(plot_handle,'XData',t_s(1:sample_index), ...
        'YData',y_m(1:sample_index))
    drawnow
    pause(0.04)
end %[output:35985f27]
%%
%[text] ## Core Validation: Check the Known Starting Value
%[text] At `t=0`, the model must return the stated starting position, 0 m. This executable assertion checks that claim using a tiny numerical tolerance. If it runs without an error, the check passes.
assert(abs(y_m(1)-y0_m) < 1e-12,'Initial-position validation failed.')
%[text] The teal marker below makes the same check visible on the graph.
hold on %[output:0d33dda7]
plot(t_s(1),y_m(1),'o','MarkerSize',8,'MarkerFaceColor',[0.00 0.50 0.45], ... %[output:0d33dda7]
    'MarkerEdgeColor',[0.00 0.50 0.45]) %[output:0d33dda7]
legend('Analytical model','Initial-value check','Location','southwest') %[output:0d33dda7]
%%
%[text] ## Working Exposure: Animate the Same Model
%[text] This lecturer-controlled animation shows the ball moving upward and downward while the position--time curve is revealed. The supplied `for` loop is only a display tool: writing loops is introduced as Core work in Week 2. The model, array, and validation are unchanged.
%[text] Run this section after the graph section. Watch how each moving-ball position corresponds to one new point on the graph.
tiledlayout(1,2,'TileSpacing','compact','Padding','compact') %[output:450b249a]
nexttile %[output:450b249a]
ball_handle = plot(0,y_m(1),'o','MarkerSize',18,'MarkerFaceColor',[0.05 0.26 0.47], ... %[output:450b249a]
    'MarkerEdgeColor',[0.05 0.26 0.47]); %[output:450b249a]
grid on %[output:450b249a]
xlim([-1 1]) %[output:450b249a]
ylim([0 22]) %[output:450b249a]
xlabel('Horizontal position (m)') %[output:450b249a]
ylabel('Vertical position, y (m)') %[output:450b249a]
title('Ball in vertical motion') %[output:450b249a]
nexttile %[output:450b249a]
trace_handle = animatedline('Color',[0.05 0.26 0.47],'LineWidth',2.5); %[output:450b249a]
grid on %[output:450b249a]
xlim([0 4]) %[output:450b249a]
ylim([0 22]) %[output:450b249a]
xlabel('Time, t (s)') %[output:450b249a]
ylabel('Vertical position, y (m)') %[output:450b249a]
title('Position--time graph revealed') %[output:450b249a]
for sample_index = 1:numel(t_s) %[output:group:11f035ce]
    set(ball_handle,'YData',y_m(sample_index)) %[output:450b249a]
    addpoints(trace_handle,t_s(sample_index),y_m(sample_index))
    drawnow
    pause(0.04)
end %[output:group:11f035ce]
%%
%[text] ## Interpret the Result
%[text] The curve rises because the initial velocity is upward. It bends downward because gravity reduces the upward velocity, reaches a highest point near 2.04 s, then returns downward. The equation, array calculation, and graph describe the same physical motion.
%%
%[text] ## Working Exposure: Selected Values and Continuous Reference
%[text] This optional section shows a compact table and the continuous analytical peak. It is useful evidence, but the Core route is already complete without it.
selected = table(t_s([1 11 21 31 41])',y_m([1 11 21 31 41])', ... %[output:group:1c4ac8b5] %[output:256e878d]
    'VariableNames',{'time_s','position_m'}) %[output:group:1c4ac8b5] %[output:256e878d]
t_peak_s = v0_mps/g_mps2 %[output:1c101607]
y_peak_m = y0_m + v0_mps^2/(2*g_mps2) %[output:436afb50]
%%
%[text] ## Reproducibility Record
%[text] Run sections from top to bottom in a fresh MATLAB session. This demonstration requires MATLAB R2025a or later and base MATLAB only. Locked inputs: `y0_m=0 m`, `v0_mps=20 m s^-1`, `g_mps2=9.81 m s^-2`, and `t_s=0:0.1:4 s`.

